Who knows if you understand this context or not, or whether 
it matters.  The original question was "Why don't we
say, of a tree, "The leaf is its".  For the answer, we
necessarily HAVE to go to the history of the language.
The answer can not be in modern usage.  So my citation
of "proper English" was an explanation of the etymology
of the word "its".  

"His" is an apostrophe-less form of "he's".  As a backformation,
we might suspect that this is what "its" is.  But it's not.
At no time was I "arguing" for the above rule, but only
supplying the history as explanation of why "its" is an
adjective and not a possessive.

> I thought we'd gone through (1) pretty well, agreeing that the
> argument was correct some time ago but not anymore.

The "argument" is still correct.  This pattern is still a true
and pervasive pattern.  Most professional writers avoid
letting inanimate objects possess things, and when they
do, they are conscious of the fact and do so deliberately
and so as to pepper their writing with the deviation.

The fact that your Google search turned up the rule 
should be evidence of that.  The examples of inanimate 
objects taking possessives were fairly animate (such as
water, which is moving and which we often assign a "spirit"
in various contexts.)  No good writer says "the desk's top"
rather than the "desk top".  The rule still holds.

 
