There is more that just saying "English is flexible".  It
has a vibrancy and life to it. There is more than one way
to be "flexible" and English is flexible (and vibrant) in
many different ways.  I've maintained that it is _one of the
best_ but I've never said that it was _the best_.  

As a counter argument, it does no good to say "Well, French
has this same property, and Japanese has this same other
property, and Latin as this same third property, so how
can you say English is superior".  Because if it takes
three languages to come up with all three properites, then
this is equivalent to saying "These three guys can beat you
up, so what makes you think you're so tough?"

Second, I didn't say English was superior period, but that 
English was superior as a Linga Franca.  As someone else
pointed out French was really good for an international
contract language and he gave the reasons why and I 
agree with those reasons.  English is, for the reasons
he gave, too twisty and squirrely to serve as well as
French for that purpose.  

English has something else that other languages don't have:
The King James Bible and Shakespeare.  Mostly Shakespeare.
Shakespeare wasn't just a master of English, but far more,
he defined what English was going to be.  To a lesser 
extent, the translators of the KJV had a similar impact on
English.  And these impacts hit 500 years ago.  Today,
when dictionary writers decide whether a word goes in, one
of the first questions is "was it in Shakespeare or KJV"? 
If "yes", in it goes.  While some people think that version
of English is really old fashioned, English has not changed
substantially, in form, for 500 years, and this has been
a tremendous unifying force.  And yet, the language has
not become stilted, but continued to absorb and grow.
We can all read Shakespeare and KJV, but it takes special
training to read Canterbury Tales or Beowulf in the original
tongue.  Shakespeare and KJV have English a "grounding" or
"center" or "anchor" which hit just when the printing press
was going great guns. So when a new dialect or creol comes
along, the anchor pulls it in.  This is part of the vibrancy
which is not destructive.

Compare this to the history of the German dialects, which
were (and are) many until Hitler decided to unify them.
And now I understand it's faddish to recover the older
dialects again.  And Germany has had the printing press
longer than the rest of us.  But where was the "anchor"
in German literature which would be the equivalent of
Shakespeare or the KJ Bible?  Luther was prolific and
wrote a lot in German, but also in Latin.  (And I'm a
huge fan of Luther and have read tons of his stuff,
but I don't see any language-anchors in his work, unless
his German translation of the Bible did something along
those lines.)


